   "title": "Instructions for Handling Plain-Language Telegrams Written in Roman Letters",
   "doc_type": "communications manual with sample telegrams",
   "start_page": 12,
   "end_page": 24,
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1945-09-21",
   "from": "Japanese Ministry of Foreign Affairs",
   "to": "Japanese diplomatic and consular communications personnel",
   "about": "A Japanese manual prescribing the Romanization, word division, numbering and formatting of economical plain-language telegrams.",
   "summary": "The document, apparently filmed in reverse internal page order, is titled “羅馬字綴平文電報取扱要領” and explains how Japanese text should be rendered in Roman letters for international telegraph transmission. It covers word division, long vowels, numerals, dates, brackets, Chinese-character explanations, combining Japanese with foreign terms, telegram and reference numbers, urgent messages, split transmissions and multiple cables sent together. Examples include a Yoshida cable concerning military events in Sichuan and a diplomatic telegram about the Stresa meeting, Germany, the Danube states and Central European security. Page 24 supplies the Japanese Legation in Panama provenance stamp dated September 21, 1945.",
